WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.140
Terraform helper scripts run with credentials.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:16.680
Those credentials may come from a cloud CLI session, a local profile, a service account, a role assumption workflow, a managed identity, workload identity, a CI/CD runner, or a secret store.

3
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:25.100
Whatever the source, the identity behind the script determines what infrastructure Terraform can read, create, update, or remove.

4
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.280
That makes credential design one of the most important parts of Terraform automation security.

5
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:37.320
Least privilege should be based on action, environment, service, and repository.

6
00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:42.940
A formatting or validation workflow should not need the same authority as a production apply.

7
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.720
A plan workflow may need enough read access to evaluate current state, but it may not need broad administrative rights.

8
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:54.980
An import or emergency recovery workflow may require a separate path with additional review.

9
00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:59.960
Separating these identities helps contain mistakes and makes access review more meaningful.

10
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:07.520
Approved runtime credential delivery is safer than hard-coded secrets, shared keys, or broad local profiles.

11
00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:18.980
In CI/CD, prefer platform-supported secret scoping, short-lived credentials, workload identity, or managed identity where those mechanisms fit the environment.

12
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:25.000
Locally, make credentials explicit enough that a script can confirm the intended account and role before taking action.

13
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:33.980
In all cases, logs should avoid raw secrets, tokens, private keys, sensitive environment values, and provider credential details.

14
00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:41.140
Broad administrator credentials may make helper scripts easy to run, but they make errors and compromise harder to contain.

15
00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:48.680
Terraform automation identities need ownership, purpose, rotation, revocation, and periodic review.

16
00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:55.820
Teams should know which scripts use which identities, what each identity can do, who owns it, and how to disable it quickly.

17
00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.600
Secure helper scripts reduce both accidental blast radius and operational uncertainty.
